note An impl block has no name in the grammar, so its name is built from the trait and type fields to read exactly as rust-analyzer reports it - 'impl Trait for Type<T>', without the block's own generic parameters. The two backends have to agree letter for letter or a step anchored to an impl under one of them is stale under the other, which is what happened to every generic impl on a cold cache.
